# Preacher

**Alignments.** Any, but generally good or selfish. Most are well-meaning,
honourable, and live by the Code of the New West.

The New West is a lawless, violent frontier where the law usually comes from
whoever has the biggest muscles and the most powerful guns. In the old West
travelling judges rode from town to town dealing frontier justice; in this one a
new kind of travelling judge walks the land, and it is the Preacher.

Preachers counsel people on right and wrong, settle disputes, speak on
another''s behalf, and sit as judges. They are tough, clever, observant and
self-reliant, and most are nomadic - riding from town to town hearing cases,
levying judgements, and offering advice and prayers. Large towns and cities
usually keep one or two resident Preachers at each of their churches.

## Two kinds

**The Fire and Brimstone Preacher** deals out hard punishment - lynchings,
floggings, beatings, tarring and feathering, hard labour - with long sermons
about evil and penance to go with them. He carries a gun, typically a Big Bore
sawed-off or an ion blaster, and has no compunction about rooting out evil with
violence. These are hard people for hard times, most of them good and sincerely
protective of the weak, and most of them grown intolerant of what they see
around them. Only about one in seven is a woman.

**The Peacemaker** is gentler and more philosophical, teaching healing,
compassion and tolerance, and appealing to hope and brotherhood among all of
god''s creatures, D-bees and aliens included. Peacemakers judge too, and will
sentence a murderer to death - but for lesser crimes they weigh the
circumstances and whether the perpetrator is truly repentant. Fire and Brimstone
Preachers often think them soft, which they are not: they stand against evil and
fight to protect the innocent when there is no other course, and many have died
doing it.

## Lore

Also known as the Reverend, the Minister, Brother, Sister, the Sermonizer and
the Bible Thumper. The book is explicit that the class is not meant to disparage
any faith, that it is a broad picture of the Judeo-Christian travelling
preachers of the Old West refitted to Rifts, and that if a religious character
makes anyone at the table uncomfortable it should simply be left out of the
game. Most ministries are regional or local, which makes for scores of similar
but divergent teachings and church laws.

A Preacher may have an assistant who acts as servant and secretary and helps
spread the word - usually a low-level rogue scholar, vagabond, saddle tramp,
saloon bum or ordinary townsperson.
